# .spool/api/api.req

feature: API

requirements:

  - id: API-1
    title: Project Loading Contract
    description: >
      LoadProject must validate the project path and the
      presence and validity of .spool.yaml before running
      the pipeline, returning a categorized *ProjectError
      for each distinct failure mode.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-05-07
    rationale: >
      The api package is the public Go-library entry point
      to Spool. Library callers — notably spool-mcp — need
      to branch on the kind of failure to map errors back
      to their own contracts: a missing config is not the
      same problem as a malformed config or a non-existent
      directory. Categorizing failures via *ProjectError
      kinds keeps that logic on the library side rather
      than asking every caller to parse error messages.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: API-1-1
        title: Absolute Path Required
        description: >
          Given a relative project path,
          when LoadProject is invoked,
          then it returns a *ProjectError with kind
          invalid_project.

      - id: API-1-2
        title: Non-Existent Path Rejected
        description: >
          Given an absolute path that does not exist on disk,
          when LoadProject is invoked,
          then it returns a *ProjectError with kind
          invalid_project.

      - id: API-1-3
        title: Missing Config Detected
        description: >
          Given a valid project directory with no .spool.yaml,
          when LoadProject is invoked,
          then it returns a *ProjectError with kind
          missing_config.

      - id: API-1-4
        title: Invalid Config Detected
        description: >
          Given a project directory containing a .spool.yaml
          that cannot be parsed,
          when LoadProject is invoked,
          then it returns a *ProjectError with kind
          invalid_config.

  - id: API-2
    title: Partial-State Tolerance
    description: >
      LoadProject must accept partial project states —
      missing reqs directory, malformed individual .req
      files, test files with no matching interpreter — by
      surfacing them as Warnings rather than failing the
      whole load.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-05-07
    rationale: >
      A freshly initialized Spool project may have a
      .spool.yaml without any .req files yet. A working
      project may temporarily contain a malformed .req file
      mid-edit, or a test file under an unexpected
      extension. Failing the entire load on any of these
      would make tools unusable during normal authoring.
      Tolerating the partial state and reporting it as a
      warning lets callers decide what to do — and lets
      spool-mcp answer queries like list_features against
      a freshly initialized project without erroring.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: API-2-1
        title: Missing Reqs Directory Loads Empty
        description: >
          Given a project with a valid .spool.yaml but no
          reqs directory on disk,
          when LoadProject is invoked,
          then it returns a non-nil Project with no
          requirements and no features, and no error.

      - id: API-2-2
        title: Malformed Req File Surfaces As Parse Warning
        description: >
          Given a project where one .req file is malformed
          and others are valid,
          when LoadProject is invoked,
          then the returned Project's Warnings includes an
          entry of kind parse identifying the malformed
          file, and requirements from the valid files are
          still present.

      - id: API-2-3
        title: Unhandled Test File Surfaces As Scan Warning
        description: >
          Given a project containing a test file that no
          interpreter handles,
          when LoadProject is invoked,
          then the returned Project's Warnings includes an
          entry of kind scan identifying the file.

  - id: API-3
    title: Project Query Surface
    description: >
      A loaded Project must expose lookup-by-id, listing,
      and diagnostic methods that return read-only views
      derived from the underlying index.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-05-07
    rationale: >
      The api package exists to give external Go callers a
      stable querying surface that does not leak internal
      types. Each method on Project corresponds to a single
      observable behavior; exhaustively pinning them in
      tests prevents accidental contract drift when the
      underlying index types are refactored.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: API-3-1
        title: Requirement Lookup Returns Requirement With ACs
        description: >
          Given a loaded Project with a requirement whose
          ACs include linked tests,
          when Requirement is called with that requirement's
          id,
          then a non-nil RequirementView is returned carrying
          its acceptance criteria with the test references
          attached to each AC.

      - id: API-3-2
        title: Requirement Lookup Returns Nil For Non-Requirement ID
        description: >
          Given a loaded Project,
          when Requirement is called with an id that does
          not match any requirement — including an id that
          matches an acceptance criterion or no entity at
          all,
          then nil is returned.

      - id: API-3-3
        title: AC Lookup Returns AC With Parent Title
        description: >
          Given a loaded Project with an AC whose parent
          requirement has a non-empty title,
          when AC is called with that AC's id,
          then a non-nil AcceptanceCriterionView is returned
          carrying the parent requirement's id and title
          along with the AC's linked tests.

      - id: API-3-4
        title: AC Lookup Returns Nil For Non-AC ID
        description: >
          Given a loaded Project,
          when AC is called with an id that does not match
          any acceptance criterion — including an id that
          matches a requirement or no entity at all,
          then nil is returned.

      - id: API-3-5
        title: Requirements Lists Sorted Without Inlined ACs
        description: >
          Given a loaded Project,
          when Requirements is called with an empty filter,
          then it returns one RequirementView per requirement
          sorted by id, and each entry has an empty
          AcceptanceCriteria slice.

      - id: API-3-6
        title: Requirements Applies Feature And Status Filters
        description: >
          Given a loaded Project containing requirements in
          multiple features and with multiple statuses,
          when Requirements is called with a non-empty
          Feature or Status filter,
          then the result contains only requirements
          matching the filter.

      - id: API-3-7
        title: Features Lists Sorted With Counts
        description: >
          Given a loaded Project with multiple features,
          when Features is called,
          then it returns one FeatureView per feature
          sorted alphabetically by name, each carrying its
          requirement count and AC count.

      - id: API-3-8
        title: Validation Errors Surface Index Errors
        description: >
          Given a loaded Project whose index detected a
          duplicate requirement id, a duplicate AC id, or
          a feature/prefix mismatch,
          when ValidationErrors is called,
          then each detected error appears as a
          ValidationError with a non-empty Message.

  - id: API-4
    title: Report Formatting
    description: >
      FormatReport must dispatch to the JSON, text, or
      markdown formatter based on the requested format
      name and reject unknown names with a clear error.
    status: active
    deciders:
      - Matt Ames
    consulted: []
    date: 2026-05-07
    rationale: >
      Three concrete formatters already exist in
      internal/reporter; FormatReport is the public
      selector callers use to pick one without importing
      internal packages. Pinning the format names and the
      unknown-name error message protects callers that
      branch on either.
    superseded_by:
    acceptance_criteria:

      - id: API-4-1
        title: JSON Format
        description: >
          Given a Report,
          when FormatReport is called with format "json",
          then it returns the same bytes as the JSON
          formatter applied directly to that Report.

      - id: API-4-2
        title: Text Format
        description: >
          Given a Report,
          when FormatReport is called with format "text",
          then it returns the same bytes as the text
          formatter applied directly to that Report.

      - id: API-4-3
        title: Markdown Format
        description: >
          Given a Report,
          when FormatReport is called with format
          "markdown",
          then it returns the same bytes as the markdown
          formatter applied directly to that Report.

      - id: API-4-4
        title: Unknown Format Rejected
        description: >
          Given a Report,
          when FormatReport is called with a format other
          than json, text, or markdown,
          then it returns a non-nil error whose message
          identifies the accepted format names.
